Executive Summary & Systemic Architecture Map
The United States financial market operates as a deterministic, four-layer infrastructure where legislative authority, regulatory policy, central depository functions, clearing mechanics, and private banking liquidity pipelines form an unbroken causal chain. A bottleneck, policy rider, or operational failure at any single layer instantaneously destabilizes downstream settlement operations.
Recent macro-level interventions—including the August 2026 SEC directive to restructure the Consolidated Audit Trail (CAT) governance by transitioning it from the SRO-led CAT NMS Plan directly to the SEC's budget via Section 31 fees, the January 2025 retrospective rescission of Staff Accounting Bulletin No. 121 (SAB 121) via SAB 122, and the July 2026 NSCC Gap Risk Measure amendments evaluating the two largest net non-diversified positions while decomposing ETFs—have fundamentally redefined systemic risk modeling. Concurrently, post-May 2024 T+1 accelerated settlement pressures and historic multi-billion-dollar Anti-Money Laundering (AML) enforcement actions have introduced new operational constraints on market intermediaries.





