Deterministic Causal Stages
Legislative Level (Layer 1 → Layer 2):
The House and Senate Appropriations Committees establish the statutory spending ceilings for the SEC via the Financial Services and General Government (FSGG) appropriations bills. Although the SEC offsets its operations through transaction fees collected under Section 31 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, the Anti-Deficiency Act (31 U.S.C. §§ 1341–1342) strictly forbids the SEC from deploying funds without explicit annual congressional authorization.
Legislative policy riders restricting the ingestion of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) into the Consolidated Audit Trail (CAT) and prolonged litigation over SRO funding structures historically impaired the SEC's surveillance capabilities. In response to recurring data opacity, in August 2026, SEC Chairman Paul Atkins formally initiated a structural overhaul: moving to rescind Rule 613, dissolve the industry-led SRO funding model (the CAT NMS Plan), and transition CAT operations directly under SEC budgetary authority funded via Section 31 fees by late 2027. This followed a March 2026 CAT NMS Plan amendment that eliminated $50M–$70M in operating expenses by purging historical data older than three years and halting interim lifecycle linkages.
Regulatory & Depository Level (Layer 2 → Layer 3):
Surveillance blindspots directly impact the custodial core: the Depository Trust Company (DTC), where all eligible securities are held in fungible bulk under the legal title of Cede & Co. Under Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) Article 8 (§§ 8-501 to 8-511), market participants do not possess direct property rights in specific underlying shares, but rather statutory "Security Entitlements" against intermediaries. This structure allows broker-dealers to legally rehypothecate customer margin securities up to 140% of the customer's debit balance under SEC Rule 15c3-3.
Until early 2025, custodial balance sheets were constrained by Staff Accounting Bulletin No. 121 (SAB 121), which mandated that digital asset custodians record client-safeguarded assets as liabilities with corresponding balance sheet assets, inflating leverage ratios under Basel III. On January 23, 2025, the SEC issued SAB 122, which formally and retrospectively rescinded SAB 121, allowing Tier-1 custodial banks to safeguard crypto-assets off-balance-sheet and expanding institutional balance sheet capacity.
Clearing & Algorithmic Level (Layer 3 → Layer 4):
When market volume or volatility spikes, trades flow into the central counterparties: the National Securities Clearing Corporation (NSCC) and the Options Clearing Corporation (OCC). The NSCC computes clearing members' daily Clearing Fund requirements using the formula:
$$\text{Required Margin} = \text{VaR Charge} + \text{Gap Risk Measure} + \text{Excess Capital Premium (ECP)} + \text{Mark-to-Market} + \text{Special Charges}$$
Value-at-Risk (VaR): Parametric and historical simulation at a 99% confidence interval over a 3-year lookback.
Enhanced Gap Risk Measure (July 2026): To prevent clearing members from evading concentration charges via proxy instruments, the SEC approved NSCC rule modifications in July 2026. The enhanced Gap Risk Measure evaluates concentration haircuts (up to 100%) on the sum of the gross market values of a member's two largest non-diversified net positions across its entire portfolio and decomposes Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs) into their underlying equity constituents.
Excess Capital Premium (ECP): Imposed under NSCC Procedure XV when a member’s calculated volatility margin component exceeds its excess regulatory net capital (SEC Rule 15c3-1), scaling margin demands non-linearly.
The Accord Interlock: When equity options are exercised at the OCC, physical stock delivery routes to the NSCC for Continuous Net Settlement (CNS). Under the restated OCC-NSCC Accord, the OCC transfers a Guaranty Substitution Payment (GSP) to the NSCC to cover incremental counterparty risk.
Participant & Settlement Chokepoint (Layer 4):
Clearing brokers receive these automated margin calls early in the trading day. Final settlement occurs at 4:15 PM ET via the Federal Reserve’s National Settlement Service (NSS), where Settling Banks enforce strict Net Debit Caps ($1.8 billion maximum per participant). If an undercapitalized clearing broker cannot satisfy an ECP or Gap Risk call, the Settling Bank issues a "Refusal to Settle," precipitating immediate broker default and triggering the NSCC Rule 4 Loss Allocation Waterfall:
Liquidate the defaulting member’s Clearing Fund deposit and collateral.
Apply the DTCC Corporate Contribution (a predefined retained-earnings capital buffer).
Mutualize all remaining losses across surviving, non-defaulting clearing participants.
To avoid insolvency and default, clearing brokers implement "Position Closing Only" (PCO) restrictions—disabling the buy button for volatile securities to artificially force their net position to zero and reduce VaR margin requirements.
