Constitutional & Statutory Framework:
Under Article I, Section 9, Clause 7 of the U.S. Constitution, no money can be drawn from the Treasury without statutory authorization. The House Committee on Appropriations and Senate Committee on Appropriations, through their Subcommittees on Financial Services and General Government (FSGG), exercise control over the SEC’s operational budget.
Section 31 Fee Collection Mechanics:
The SEC collects transaction fees under Section 31 of the Exchange Act, which are classified as offsetting collections. If Congress sets the SEC's budget ceiling below projected Section 31 collections, the excess funds are absorbed into the General Fund of the U.S. Treasury rather than supporting regulatory operations.
The 2026 CAT Governance Pivot:
Historically, CAT was governed by a consortium of Self-Regulatory Organizations (SROs) under the CAT NMS Plan. In August 2026, SEC Chairman Atkins directed the agency to overhaul CAT by rescinding Rule 613 and transferring full operational and financial control directly to the SEC, supported by dedicated Section 31 transaction fee allocations. This structural shift addresses cost overruns, SRO funding disputes, and data-security concerns regarding PII ingestion.
