Continuous Net Settlement (CNS) Novation:
Upon trade matching, the NSCC novates the transaction, interposing itself as the central counterparty. The CNS engine nets each clearing member’s buy and sell obligations across all market venues into a single net-long or net-short position per CUSIP, reducing gross settlement obligations by 97% to 99%.
The OCC-NSCC Accord & Guaranty Substitution Payment (GSP):
When equity options are exercised or assigned at the OCC, the underlying share delivery obligations transfer to the NSCC's CNS engine. Under the restated OCC-NSCC Accord, if a common clearing member defaults, the OCC transfers a Guaranty Substitution Payment (GSP) to the NSCC to cover the incremental net risk exposure.
The July 2026 Enhanced Gap Risk Measure:
Approved by the SEC in July 2026, the updated NSCC Gap Risk methodology calculates concentration haircuts on the sum of the gross market values of a member's two largest non-diversified net positions (scaling up to 100%). It also decomposes ETFs to calculate indirect single-stock exposures, preventing members from circumventing concentration charges via basket trading.
NSCC Rule 4 Loss Allocation Waterfall:
In the event of a member default:
Defaulter Resources: Seizure and liquidation of the defaulting member’s Clearing Fund deposits and eligible collateral.
Corporate Contribution: Deployment of the DTCC Corporate Contribution (a predefined tranche of DTCC retained earnings).
Loss Mutualization: Pro-rata loss allocation across all surviving non-defaulting clearing participants based on their 24-month average Clearing Fund contributions.
